summary:
- topic: Superintendent Search Finalists
  text: The South Portland School Board announced two finalists for the superintendent
    of schools position. A community meet-and-greet event was scheduled for May 6th
    at South Portland Middle School to allow the public to interact with both candidates.
- topic: Community Engagement Findings
  text: District principals presented findings from community listening sessions and
    surveys regarding the elementary school reconfiguration. The feedback highlighted
    a deep tension between staff and families prioritizing equity-driven changes and
    those advocating for the preservation of neighborhood schools.
- topic: FY2027 Budget Approval
  text: The Board approved the final FY2027 operating budget with a 6% tax increase.
    The budget was significantly aided by the City Council's agreement to forgive
    up to $2 million of the projected deficit from the city's fund balance, avoiding
    a costly repayment plan.
- topic: Personnel Reductions and Restorations
  text: The Board finalized personnel reductions and restorations for the upcoming
    school year. Utilizing one-time Maine Supplemental Funding and insurance savings,
    the district restored four teaching positions on a one-time basis, alongside two
    special education ed techs and one custodial position.
- topic: Reconfiguration Timeline Delay
  text: In a 5-1 vote, the Board adopted a resolution to delay the implementation
    of the PK-4 elementary school grade reconfiguration to the 2027-2028 school year.
    The delay aims to provide the newly formed Steering Committee and school administration
    with sufficient time for thoughtful planning, boundary redrawing, and community
    building.
- topic: Public Criticism of Superintendent Search
  text: During public comment, numerous citizens, teachers, and administrators strongly
    criticized the Board's decision to conduct a closed superintendent search process.
    Speakers argued that excluding key stakeholders from the search committee devalued
    the community's voice and damaged public trust.
